<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumazawa Banzan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1619">1619</a>–<a href="/years/1691">1691</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumazawa Banzan was a Japanese Confucian scholar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-yangming">Wang Yangming</a> school and a pupil of Nakae Tōju. In the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Ikeda Mitsumasa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> Domain he advised on government, flood control and famine relief, and helped shape the domain's reforms. His later writings criticised shogunal policy, and he spent his last years under house arrest at Koga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumazawa-banzan.jpg" alt="Kumazawa Banzan" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: photo by Reggaeman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumazawa_Banzan_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kumazawa Banzan was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, the eldest of six children of Nojiri Tōbei Kazutoshi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> who had served two daimyō. His mother, Kamejo, was the daughter of Kumazawa Morihisa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yorifusa">Tokugawa Yorifusa</a> of Mito. At the age of eight he was adopted by this grandfather and took the name Kumazawa. In 1634 he became a page to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Ikeda Mitsumasa</a> of the Okayama Domain, but left the Ikeda household for a time and went back to his grandfather's home at Kirihara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> in 1645, where Mitsumasa, drawn to Yōmeigaku, valued him as Tōju's pupil. He taught at the domain school Hanabatake Kyōjō, received 300 koku in 1647 and in 1650 was put at the head of a company of gunners. In 1651 he drafted the rules of the Hanazonokai, the first form of Shizutani Gakkō, which opened in 1670 as the first school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> for commoners. After the floods and famine of 1654 he worked with Tsuda Nagatada on relief, aid to small farmers and works on mountains and rivers.</p><p>His reforms set the domain's elders against him, and as a follower of Yōmeigaku rather than the official Shushigaku he was attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshina-masayuki">Hoshina Masayuki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan">Hayashi Razan</a>. Under this pressure he left the domain in 1657. He opened a private school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1658, was driven from the city by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s officials, and from 1669 was kept under the watch of Matsudaira Nobuyuki of Akashi. In 1683 he refused an invitation from the tairō Hotta Masatoshi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his writings, among them Shūgi Washo and Daigaku Wakumon, he attacked sankin-kōtai, the ban on arms outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> class and the hereditary system, and argued for government by merit. He died in 1691 inside Koga Castle at the age of 74 and was buried at Keienji in Koga. In the Bakumatsu period his ideas were taken up by Fujita Tōko and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a>, Katsu Kaishū called him a hero in Confucian robes, and in 1910 the Meiji government gave him the Upper Fourth Rank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumazawa Banzan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mitsumasa, a committed Confucian, employed the scholar <b>Kumazawa Banzan</b>, promoted flood control and land reclamation, restricted Buddhist temples and in 1670 founded the Shizutani school, open to commoners.</blockquote><a href="/state/okayama-domain">Okayama Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1665" aria-label="Kyoto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kumazawa Banzan"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4013 4510h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4013 4510h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1665">What was here in 1665? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
